      "text": "Given the quadratic equation \\[ax^2 + bx + d = 0,\\] and the roots \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\), we can apply Vieta's formulas to this equation. Vieta's formulas provide the relationship between the roots of a polynomial and its coefficients.\n\n1. For a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + d = 0\\) with roots \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\beta \\),\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + \\beta = -\\frac{b}{a},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\alpha \\beta = \\frac{d}{a}.\n   \\]\n\n2. With roots \\( 3 \\) and \\(-2 \\), apply these formulas:\n   \\[\n   3 + (-2) = \\frac{b}{a}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3 \\cdot (-2) = \\frac{d}{a}\n   \\]\n\n3. Solving for \\( \\frac{b}{a} \\):\n   \\[\n   1 = \\frac{b}{a}\n   \\]\n   As a result, \n   \\[\n   \\frac{b}{a} = 1.\n   \\]\n\n4. For the cubic equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\), the sum of the roots (with the root \\(0\\) counted once):\n   \\[\n   0 + 3 + (-2) + r = -\\frac{c}{a}\n   \\]\n   where \\( r \\) is the third root. Therefore,\n   \\[\n   1 + r = -\\frac{c}{a}\n   \\]\n\n5. Using Vieta's formula again for the sum of all roots of the original polynomial (where degree of the polynomial indicates the total root count):\n   \\[\n   3 + (-2) + r + s = 0 \\quad \\text{(roots of cubic)} = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1 + r + s = 0\n   \\]\n   where \\( s \\) is the third root.\n\n6. Solving for \\( r + s \\):\n   \\[\n   1 + r + s = 0 \\rightarrow 1 + r + s = 0 \\rightarrow r + s = -1.\n   \\]\n\n7. From the cubic, we know:\n   \\[\n   1 + s = -\\frac{c}{a}\n   \\]\n   But since \\(s\\) is one of the roots, solving directly:\n   \\[\n   s = -1 \\quad \\text{(evidenced by balance of equation)}\n   \\]\n\n8. Backtracking, solve for \\(r\\):\n   \\[\n   r = 0 + s \\rightarrow r = s\n   \\]\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   r = -1\n   \\]\n\n9. Substitute \\( r = -1 \\) in \\(\\alpha \\beta + \\alpha \\gamma + \\beta \\gamma = -\\frac{c}{a}\\):\n   \\[\n   (3)(-2) + 3(-1) + (-2)(-1) = -\\frac{c}{a}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -6 - 3 + 2 = -\\frac{c}{a}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -7 = -\\frac{c}{a}\n   \\]\n\n10. Solving for \\(\\frac{c}{a}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{c}{a} = 7\n   \\]\n\n11. Given that we need \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b+c}{a} = 1 + 7 = 8\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]",
